e go through again step by step to make sure I didn't make any wrong assumptions, especially regarding the gravel and pitch relationship. \n\nFirst, verifying the gravel and pitch per truckload. The problem says: \"Each truckload of asphalt uses two bags of gravel and five times as many bags of gravel as it does barrels of pitch to make.\" \n\nSo the key is to parse this sentence. It might be structured as: Each truckload uses two bags of gravel, and [the number of] bags of gravel is five times as many as barrels of pitch. Therefore, G = 2 and G = 5P => P = 2/5. \n\nAlternatively, if someone misreads it as each truckload uses two bags of gravel and five times as many barrels of pitch as bags of gravel, that would be different. But the wording says \"five times as many bags of gravel as it does barrels of pitch\". So \"as many bags of gravel as barrels of pitch\", but five times. So bags of gravel = 5 * barrels of pitch. So if bags of gravel is 2, then barrels of pitch is 2/5. \n\nAnother way: If they had said \"five times as many barrels of pitch as bags of gravel\", that would be P = 5G. But since it says \"bags of gravel... five times as many... as barrels of pitch\", it's G = 5P. \n\nYes, so that part seems okay. \n\nThen, per mile, three truckloads. So 3 truckloads per mile. \n\nFirst day: 4 miles => 4 * 3 = 12 truckloads.\n\nSecond day: \"one mile less than double that\"—double of 4 is 8, less 1 is 7 miles. 7 * 3 = 21 truckloads. \n\nTotal truckloads for two days: 12 + 21 = 33 truckloads. \n\nTotal road is 16 miles, so total truckloads needed is 16 * 3 = 48 truckloads. \n\nTherefore, remaining truckloads on third day: 48 - 33 = 15 truckloads. \n\nEach truckload needs 2/5 barrels of pitch. So 15 * 2 /5 = 30 /5 = 6. \n\nYes, that's consistent. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can compute the remaining miles first. 16 - 4 -7 = 5 miles. 5 miles * 3 truckloads per mile = 15 truckloads. Each truckload needs 2/5 barrels of pitch. 15 * 2/5 = 6. \n\nTherefore, 6 barrels of pi