n later it says, \"When Kevin puts his laptop and work papers in his briefcase, it is twice the weight of Karen’s tote.\" Wait, now Kevin's briefcase with laptop and papers is twice the weight of Karen’s tote. But earlier, Karen’s tote (empty) is twice the weight of Kevin’s briefcase (empty). So Karen's tote is heavier when empty than Kevin's briefcase when empty. Then when Kevin fills his briefcase, it becomes heavier than Karen's tote. \n\nBut then the problem says, \"Karen’s tote weighs 8 pounds.\" Wait, so does that 8 pounds refer to Karen's tote when it's empty or when it's full? Because if Karen's tote is twice the weight of Kevin's empty briefcase, and if Karen's tote is 8 pounds, then that would be when her tote is empty? Or maybe not? Wait, the problem says \"Karen’s work tote bag is twice the weight of her husband Kevin’s briefcase when the briefcase is empty.\" So the comparison is between Karen's tote (probably as it is, maybe empty) and Kevin's empty briefcase. Then later, when Kevin fills his briefcase, it becomes twice the weight of Karen’s tote. So Karen's tote here is still presumably empty? Or does Karen's tote also have stuff in it? The problem doesn't mention Karen's tote having contents. So maybe we can assume Karen's tote is empty? But the problem says \"Karen’s tote weighs 8 pounds.\" So maybe that's the weight of her tote when it's empty? Wait, but people usually carry work tote bags with stuff inside. Hmm. The problem is a bit ambiguous here. Wait, let me read again.\n\nOriginal problem: \"Karen’s work tote bag is twice the weight of her husband Kevin’s briefcase when the briefcase is empty. When Kevin puts his laptop and work papers in his briefcase, it is twice the weight of Karen’s tote. Kevin’s work papers are a sixth of the weight of the contents of his full briefcase. If Karen’s tote weighs 8 pounds, how many more pounds does Kevin’s laptop weigh than Karen’s tote?\"\n\nSo let me parse each sentence.\n\n1. Karen’s work tote bag is twic