erefore, she has to buy exactly 14. Wait, but the problem says she can buy packs and individual bags. So if she buys 3 packs (15 bags) but only needs 14, she can't return them, so she has to buy that extra one. But the problem states she needs to buy every student the theme they requested. So she can't have extra bags of the same theme? Wait, no. If she buys 15 pumpkin bags, she can just give out 14 to the students and have one left over. The problem doesn't say she can't have extras, only that she has to buy every student the theme they requested. So having an extra bag is allowed. Therefore, buying 3 packs for pumpkin is acceptable and cheaper than buying 2 packs and 4 individual. So total cost is 7 + 9 = 16. \n\nBut the ground truth is 17. That suggests that maybe my reasoning is wrong. Let me check again. Maybe there's something I didn't consider. \n\nWait, let me check if buying different combinations for vampire-themed bags. For 11 vampire bags, is there a better way? For example, buying 1 pack of 5 and then 6 individual? That would be 1*3 + 6*1 = 3 + 6 = $9, which is worse than $7. If she buys 2 packs (10) + 1 individual, which is $7. So that's better. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a way to have less total cost if she adjusts the number of packs and individual bags for both themes? Wait, maybe there's a combined optimization? For example, if buying more packs for one theme allows her to buy fewer individual bags for another? But since the themes are separate, packs are separate, I don't think so. The packs are specific to each theme. So packs of vampire and packs of pumpkin are separate. Therefore, the optimization for each theme is independent. \n\nBut then why is the answer 17? Maybe my mistake is in the pumpkin count? Let me check again. If she needs 14 pumpkin. Let me think of other ways. For example, if she buys 2 packs (10) and 4 individual, total is 14. Cost is 2*3 + 4*1 = 6 + 4 = 10. Alternatively, if she buys 3 packs (15) and has one extra, costing 9. So 9 is better. So total is 7 + 9 = 16. \n\nWait, unless there's a restriction that you can't buy more than needed? But the problem didn't say that. The teacher is allowed to have extra bags, right? The problem says she needs to buy every student the theme they requested. So she needs to have at least that number. But since the packs come in 5s, sometimes you have to buy more. \n\nWait, but maybe there's a different way to combine the purchases? For example, maybe buying some individual bags instead of a pack if it's cheaper? Let me check. \n\nFor vampire: 11 bags. Let me consider different numbers of packs:\n\n- 0 packs: 11 individual. 11*1 = $11. Worse than $7.\n\n- 1 pack (5): 5 + 6 individual. Cost: 3 + 6 = $9. Worse than $7.\n\n- 2 packs (10): 10 + 1 individual. Cost: 6 + 1 = $7. Best so far.\n\n- 3 packs (15): 15 bags, cost 9. But since you can't return, you have 4 extra. But 9 is more than 7, so worse.\n\nSo vampire is definitely $7.\n\nFor pumpkin:\n\n- 0 packs: 14 individual. $14. Not good.\n\n- 1 pack (5): 5 + 9 individual. 3 + 9 = $12.\n\n- 2 packs (10): 10 + 4 individual. 6 + 4 = $10.\n\n- 3 packs (15): 15 bags. 3*3= $9. \n\nSo pumpkin is $9. Total is 7 + 9 = 16. \n\nBut according to the problem statement, the correct answer is 17. So either the problem has a different correct answer, or my reasoning is wrong. Wait, maybe I miscalculated something?\n\nWait, let me check the original problem again. \n\nThe store sells packs of 5 of each theme at $3 per package, and individual bags at $1 each. The teacher needs 11 vampire and 14 pumpkin. \n\nWait, let me check if buying 3 packs for vampire. 3 packs would be 15, costing $9. But she needs only 11, so 4 extra. But maybe if she does that, and for pumpkin she can buy less? But no, since pumpkin is separate. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there is a constraint that the teacher cannot buy more than needed? But the problem says she has to buy every student the theme they requested. It doesn't say she can't have ext