to walk to the Nile Delta\". So the duration of the return journey was Paul's original time (4h) plus 2h, so 6h. \n\nTherefore, the calculation seems correct. The combined time is 4 + 7*6 = 4 + 42 = 46. \n\nBut let me check again if there are other journeys. Did Paul make any other trips? The problem says Paul walked from home to Delta (4h), then on the return journey (Delta to home) with six others, which took 6h. So only two trips total. \n\nAre there any other alligators besides Paul and the six on the return trip? The problem says Paul traveled with six other alligators on the return journey. So during the first trip, only Paul was traveling. During the return trip, Paul plus six others. Therefore, total alligators involved are Paul and six others, but Paul is already counted in the return trip. \n\nBut for combined time, we need to count all the individual times. So Paul walked both trips, right? He first went alone, then came back with the others. So Paul's total time is 4h + 6h = 10h. Then, each of the six other alligators only walked on the return trip, which took 6h each. Therefore, their total time is 6 alligators * 6h = 36h. Then total combined time would be Paul's 10h + 36h = 46h. \n\nWait, so this is another way to think about it. Previously, I considered the first trip as 1*4h and the return trip as 7*6h, which totals 4 + 42 = 46h. This way, it's Paul's total (10h) plus others' total (36h) = 46h. Same answer. \n\nTherefore, both methods confirm the answer is 46. \n\nBut let me make sure there's no trick here. For example, maybe the six other alligators also made a trip to the Delta before? But the problem says Paul took 4 hours to walk to the Delta. On the return journey, he traveled with six others. It doesn't mention the six others going to the Delta first. So it's likely they started from the Delta and went back to the home. \n\nTherefore, only Paul had walked both ways, while the other six only walked on the return trip. Therefore, total time is 4 