d your legs, doubling up would mean putting two bands. But she bought two bands. If she doubles up both sets, maybe she's using both bands in each movement? \n\nThis part is a bit confusing. Let's think again. The problem says: \"she doubles up both sets of bands and places them around her legs\". So maybe \"both sets\" refers to the two bands. If each band is a set, then doubling up each set would mean using each band twice? Or maybe doubling up the bands means using two bands together. For example, if you have two bands, and you put them both around your legs, then the total resistance would be the sum of both bands. \n\nBut each band adds 5 pounds. If you use two bands, that's 5 + 5 = 10 pounds. But if you double up, maybe you're using each band in a way that their resistance is doubled. For example, if you stretch a band twice as much, the resistance doubles? But that might depend on how the bands are used. However, typically, when you double up bands, meaning using two bands together, the resistance adds up. So if each band is 5 pounds, two bands would be 10 pounds. \n\nBut the problem says \"doubles up both sets of bands\". Maybe she has two bands, and she doubles each one. If each band is doubled, then each contributes 5 * 2 = 10 pounds? Then two bands would be 10 + 10 = 20? Then adding the 10-pound dumbbell would be 20 + 10 = 30. Which matches the ground truth answer. But how to get there?\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"doubling up\" is a term specific to bands. From what I remember, when you double up resistance bands, you either use two bands together, which adds their resistance, or you loop a band twice, which might also double the resistance. \n\nIf she has two bands and doubles up both sets, maybe she's using each band doubled. So each band is contributing 10 pounds (since doubled), and there are two bands. So 10 + 10 = 20. Then adding the 10-pound dumbbell gives 30. \n\nBut if \"doubling up both sets\" means that she's using both bands together (i.e., each