quence seems to be:\n\n- Home game 1: Not mentioned? Wait, only the last home game is mentioned. Hmm.\n\nWait, hold on. The problem says: \"Their team scored twice as many points at their last home game than their first away game.\" So the last home game score is twice the first away game. Let me define variables for each game.\n\nLet me define:\n\nLet’s denote:\n\n- Let H be the score of the last home game. Given H = 62.\n\n- Let A1 be the score of the first away game.\n\n- Let A2 be the score of the second away game.\n\n- Let A3 be the score of the third away game.\n\n- Let N be the score needed in the next game (which would be the fourth away game? Or another home game? The problem says \"their next game,\" but since the last mentioned was the third away game, maybe the next game is the fourth away game? The problem doesn't specify whether the next game is home or away, but maybe it's just the next in the sequence. Since the problem is about cumulative points, we need to consider all games mentioned so far plus the next one.\n\nFirst, according to the problem:\n\n\"Their team scored twice as many points at their last home game than their first away game.\" So H = 2*A1. Since H is 62, then A1 = H / 2 = 62 / 2 = 31. So first away game is 31 points.\n\n\"At the second away game, they scored eighteen points more than the previous away game.\" So A2 = A1 + 18. Since A1 is 31, A2 = 31 + 18 = 49.\n\n\"At the third away game, they scored two more points than at their second.\" So A3 = A2 + 2 = 49 + 2 = 51.\n\nSo far, we have:\n\n- Last home game: 62\n\n- First away game: 31\n\n- Second away game: 49\n\n- Third away game: 51\n\nNow, the question is asking: How many points do they have to score in their next game to bring their cumulative points up to four times the score from their last home game?\n\nFirst, let's compute four times the last home game score: 4*62 = 248.\n\nNow, we need to calculate the current cumulative points before the next game, and then find out what N is such that total cumulative + N = 248.\n\nBut wait, which games are included in the cumulative points? The problem says \"cumulative points,\" so we need to know which games have been played already.\n\nThe problem mentions:\n\n- Last home game\n\n- First away game\n\n- Second away game\n\n- Third away game\n\nIs the last home game separate from the away games? So, the team has played:\n\n1. First away game\n\n2. Second away game\n\n3. Third away game\n\nPlus the last home game. Wait, but when is the last home game in the sequence? Is it before or after the away games? The problem says \"their last home game\" and then talks about \"their first away game,\" \"second away game,\" etc. So maybe the sequence is:\n\nThey had a last home game, then three away games? Or is the last home game separate?\n\nWait, maybe there are multiple home games, but only the last one is mentioned. However, the problem doesn't specify how many home games there are. It only mentions the last home game and three away games.\n\nBut for cumulative points, we need to consider all games played up to now. The problem is a bit ambiguous on whether the last home game is in addition to the three away games or if the away games happened before or after.\n\nBut let me think again. The problem says:\n\n\"Her team scored twice as many points at their last home game than their first away game. At the second away game, they scored eighteen points more than the previous away game. At the third away game, they scored two more points than at their second. If Lola’s team scored 62 points at their last home game...\"\n\nSo the order is:\n\n- First away game.\n\n- Second away game (eighteen more than first away).\n\n- Third away game (two more than second away).\n\n- Last home game is twice the first away game. So the last home game could be after the three away games? Or maybe before?\n\nBut since it's called the \"last home game,\" it's probably after the first away games. So the order of games could be: