onal person? Or maybe the teacher is in addition?\n\nThis is a bit confusing. Let me try to parse the exact wording. The problem says \"calculate the total number of people on the bus considering the teacher also drives together with the students on the bus.\" So maybe the teacher is part of the people on the bus, so we need to include the teacher. If the bus already has a driver and an assistant, then adding the teacher would make it three adults. However, if the teacher is acting as the driver, then maybe the original driver is not there? But the problem says the bus \"has a driver and an assistant,\" so those are there regardless. So maybe the teacher is in addition. Therefore, driver, assistant, teacher, plus students.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the teacher is one of the driver or assistant? For example, maybe Mr. Gordon is the teacher, and he is driving the bus, so he is the driver. Then, the assistant is another person. So in that case, the driver is the teacher, so total adults would be driver (teacher) + assistant = 2 people. Therefore, total people would be students + 2 adults.\n\nBut then the problem says \"the teacher also drives together with the students on the bus.\" So maybe the teacher is driving, so he is on the bus. But if the bus already has a driver, then there would be two drivers? That seems odd. Maybe the teacher is the driver. So the original statement \"their bus has a driver and an assistant\" would then include the teacher as the driver. Therefore, the teacher is the driver, so total adults are driver (teacher) + assistant = 2. Then total people would be 50 + 70 + 2 = 122. But the given answer is 123. Hmm. So this is conflicting.\n\nWait, maybe the problem is in the translation or wording. Let me check again. The problem says:\n\n\"their bus has a driver and an assistant, and the total number of boys on the trip is 50... calculate the total number of people on the bus considering the teacher also drives together with the students on the bus.\